What a delta file contains
A walkthrough of 2023-10-24/delta.ttl, the delta for the 2023-10-24 snapshot. It carries three kinds of resources:
Change events (psgc:event/{date}/{seq}) — typed org:ChangeEvent. Each carries the snapshot date (dct:date), a one-line human description (dct:description), the change type (org:classification → psgc:concept/change-type/{type}), and the entities involved via org:originalOrganization (entity removed/renamed-from) and/or org:resultingOrganization (entity created/renamed-to). For example, a code change:
&lt;https://psgc.gov.ph/event/2023-10-24/0082&gt; a org:ChangeEvent ;
    dct:date "2023-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "PSGC code changed from 1380300033 to 1381500036"@en ;
    org:classification &lt;https://psgc.gov.ph/concept/change-type/code_change&gt; ;
    org:originalOrganization &lt;https://psgc.gov.ph/entity/1380300033&gt; ;
    org:resultingOrganization &lt;https://psgc.gov.ph/entity/1381500036&gt; .
A creation carries only org:resultingOrganization; a deletion carries only org:originalOrganization.
Entities (psgc:entity/{code}) — the units touched by the delta. The 10-digit PSGC code and the 9-digit correspondence code (identity that survives re-codings) are both recorded on org:identifier, the name on skos:prefLabel, and population on schema:population:
&lt;https://psgc.gov.ph/entity/1381500036&gt; a org:OrganizationalUnit ;
    dct:date "2023-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
    schema1:population 15103 ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rizal"@en ;
    org:classification &lt;https://psgc.gov.ph/concept/geographic-level/Bgy&gt;,
        &lt;https://psgc.gov.ph/concept/urban-rural/U&gt; ;
    org:identifier "137602033"^^xsd:string,
        "1381500036"^^xsd:string,
        "137602033"^^psgc:correspondence-code .
Concepts (psgc:concept/{scheme}/{value}) — skos:Concept nodes for the controlled vocabularies used in the delta: change-type/ (creation, deletion, code_change, …), geographic-level/ (Bgy, Mun, …) and urban-rural/ (Urban/Rural), some with skos:prefLabel labels.
This snapshot’s 92 events break down as 29 creations, 55 deletions, and 11 code changes — a single quarter’s worth of barangay creation/deletion/re-coding events.
